/* V67 — you cannot plan for something you cannot see yet.

   V63 made where you tap a decision and V64 arranged hostiles into shapes worth reading, and then
   left the player no time to read them. Hostiles are created above the top edge and fall in, which
   means a formation is invisible for its first second or so and then simply *is there*, already
   descending, already needing an answer. The decision the last two parts built exists in a window
   the player never gets to use.

   That is also a fair share of what "the game doesn't look good" describes. Things that appear
   without warning read as random even when they are not; the same arrivals, announced, read as
   authored. Arcade games have solved this the same way for forty years — the marker at the edge of
   the screen that says something is coming and where.

   One small chevron per incoming hostile, on a line just under the HUD, at the x it will enter at.
   It fades in as the hostile approaches and out as it crosses, so at rest the strip is empty. The
   chevrons of a formation are spaced exactly as its members are, which means the shape is legible
   before it arrives: three evenly spread marks is a row, two tight pairs is a keystone, one mark
   that stays is a column. A sealed member's chevron is drawn in the shell colour, so the hardest
   question a formation is going to ask is the first thing the player learns about it.

   Not the same thing as V35's incoming silhouettes, which are worth distinguishing because they
   look superficially like this and answer a different question. Those are atmosphere: four or six
   shapes drifting through the haze during the hush, deliberately unshootable, at positions that have
   nothing to do with anything real, existing to give the held breath something to hold. This is
   information about actual hostiles at their actual x, and it runs while the arena is live.

   Deliberately not a countdown bar, not a silhouette, not a name. The screen already carries a wave
   bar, a boss bar, a combo readout, two ability buttons, a weapon chip and an avatar, and the last
   part that added chrome to this arena had to be cut back a week later. A chevron is the least this
   can be and still be information. */

const V67={
 /* The strip sits below the HUD block rather than at the very top of the canvas: the canvas is the
    full screen and the wave bar, avatar and pause button are drawn over its first hundred points.
    A marker up there would be behind them. */
 y:()=>isMobile?124:96,
 /* How far above the top edge a hostile starts being announced. Beyond this the warning would be
    permanent -- something is nearly always on its way -- and a permanent warning is wallpaper. */
 lead:230,
 size:8,
 /* Photographed at .62 with a squared falloff the marks were barely there -- about 0.3 alpha at the
    distance a hostile spends most of its approach at, against a lit skyline and a planet. A warning
    that has to be looked for is not a warning. Brighter, with a floor, so a mark is legible the
    whole time it is up and merely brightest just before it matters. */
 alpha:.82
};

const v67Draw=draw;
draw=function(){
 v67Draw();
 if(!game.running||game.paused)return;

 const world=worlds[game.world%worlds.length];
 const y=V67.y();
 ctx.save();
 ctx.lineWidth=2.4;
 ctx.lineCap='round';
 ctx.lineJoin='round';
 for(const o of game.orbs){
  /* Measured against the strip, not against the top of the canvas. The canvas is the whole screen
     and the HUD covers its first hundred points, so a hostile at y=60 exists, is on screen by the
     arithmetic, and is behind the wave bar where nobody can see it. Cutting the marker at y=0 left
     a gap where neither the chevron nor the hostile was visible. It runs until the hostile actually
     reaches the line the marker is drawn on, and then hands over. */
  const d=y-o.y;
  if(d<=0||d>V67.lead)continue;
  /* Brightest just before it crosses, so the strip has a rhythm rather than a constant hum, and the
     eye is drawn at the moment the information is worth acting on. */
  const near=1-d/V67.lead;
  ctx.globalAlpha=V67.alpha*(.4+near*.6);
  ctx.strokeStyle=o.v65Plates>0?V65.shell:world.accent;
  const s=V67.size;
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.moveTo(o.x-s,y-s*.6);
  ctx.lineTo(o.x,y+s*.6);
  ctx.lineTo(o.x+s,y-s*.6);
  ctx.stroke();
 }
 ctx.restore();
};
